## Experiment 6: Graph Coloring Using Greedy Method

**Q1. What is Graph Coloring?**

Ans: Graph Coloring is the process of assigning colors to vertices such that no two adjacent vertices have the same color.

**Q2. Which algorithm technique is used in this experiment?**

Ans: Greedy Method.

**Q3. What is the objective of Graph Coloring?**

Ans: To assign colors to vertices without conflicts, ideally using the minimum possible number of colors.

**Q4. What is a chromatic number?**

Ans: The minimum number of colors required to properly color a graph.

**Q5. What is proper vertex coloring?**

Ans: A coloring in which no two adjacent vertices have the same color.

**Q6. What are adjacent vertices?**

Ans: Two vertices connected directly by an edge.

**Q7. Can non-adjacent vertices have the same color?**

Ans: Yes.

**Q8. Can adjacent vertices have the same color?**

Ans: No.

**Q9. What is the Greedy Method?**

Ans: An algorithm design technique that selects a locally optimal choice at each step.

**Q10. How does Greedy Graph Coloring work?**

Ans: It assigns the smallest available color to each vertex without conflicting with previously colored adjacent vertices.

**Q11. Does Greedy Graph Coloring always produce an optimal solution?**

Ans: No. It may use more colors than the chromatic number.

**Q12. What affects the number of colors used by the greedy algorithm?**

Ans: The order in which vertices are processed.

**Q13. How is the graph represented in our program?**

Ans: Using an adjacency matrix.

**Q14. What does zero represent in the adjacency matrix?**

Ans: The absence of an edge.

**Q15. What does one represent in the adjacency matrix?**

Ans: The presence of an edge.

**Q16. What is the purpose of the color array?**

Ans: To store the color assigned to each vertex.

**Q17. Why is the color array initialized to -1?**

Ans: To indicate that vertices are initially uncolored.

**Q18. What is the purpose of the unavailable array?**

Ans: To identify colors already used by adjacent vertices.

**Q19. Why is the unavailable array reset for each vertex?**

Ans: Because every vertex may have a different set of adjacent vertices and unavailable colors.

**Q20. What is the time complexity of our implementation?**

Ans: O(V^2).

**Q21. What is the auxiliary space complexity?**

Ans: O(V).

**Q22. What is the total space complexity including the adjacency matrix?**

Ans: O(V^2).

**Q23. What is a complete graph?**

Ans: A graph in which every pair of distinct vertices is connected by an edge.

**Q24. How many colors are required for a complete graph with N vertices?**

Ans: N colors.

**Q25. How many colors are required for a nonempty graph without edges?**

Ans: One color.

**Q26. What is a bipartite graph?**

Ans: A graph whose vertices can be divided into two disjoint sets such that every edge connects vertices from different sets.

**Q27. How many colors are required for a bipartite graph containing at least one edge?**

Ans: Two colors.

**Q28. What is the chromatic number of a triangle graph?**

Ans: Three.

**Q29. What is the chromatic number of an even cycle?**

Ans: Two.

**Q30. What is the chromatic number of an odd cycle?**

Ans: Three.

**Q31. What is the chromatic number of a tree containing at least two vertices?**

Ans: Two.

**Q32. What is the maximum degree of a graph?**

Ans: The maximum number of edges incident on any vertex in a simple graph.

**Q33. What is the upper bound on the number of colors used by greedy coloring?**

Ans: Maximum vertex degree plus one.

**Q34. What is a self-loop?**

Ans: An edge connecting a vertex to itself.

**Q35. Can a graph containing a self-loop have a proper vertex coloring?**

Ans: No.

**Q36. What is the Four Color Theorem?**

Ans: Every planar graph without self-loops can be properly colored using at most four colors.

**Q37. What is a planar graph?**

Ans: A graph that can be drawn on a plane without edges crossing except at their endpoints.

**Q38. How is Graph Coloring used in examination scheduling?**

Ans: Subjects with common students are represented as adjacent vertices and assigned different examination time slots.

**Q39. How is Graph Coloring used in compilers?**

Ans: It is used in register allocation to assign different registers to variables whose live ranges interfere.

**Q40. What is the difference between Greedy Graph Coloring and Backtracking Graph Coloring?**

Ans: Greedy coloring assigns colors without reconsidering previous choices, whereas backtracking explores alternative assignments when conflicts occur.

**Q41. Can backtracking find an optimal graph coloring?**

Ans: Yes, by systematically testing color assignments and determining the smallest feasible number of colors.

**Q42. What is the main advantage of Greedy Graph Coloring?**

Ans: It is simple and computationally efficient.

**Q43. What is the main disadvantage of Greedy Graph Coloring?**

Ans: It does not guarantee the minimum number of colors.

**Q44. How many colors are used in our first test case?**

Ans: Three colors.

**Q45. What are the applications of Graph Coloring?**

Ans: Examination scheduling, register allocation, map coloring, frequency assignment and resource allocation.
